\code{given = x == 1 & z > 0}. The event cannot mention a variable that
\code{given} fixes to a value.

A combination of variables can be given a value too, which slices the
distribution: \code{prob(d, x > 60, given = x + y == 200)} is the probability
that \eqn{X} exceeds 60 when \eqn{X + Y = 200}. This needs the
distribution's \code{linear} property, as for events on combinations.
